-A coordinator can hand its tenure to a fresh session of the same agent without a person in between. `coordinator ready --note FILE` freezes the caller's tenure: herdr copies the note into its state directory and records its sha256. Run `ready` inside the turn and end the turn afterwards. While frozen, every mutation sent from the tenure's pane is refused with `coordinator_frozen`: `coordinator handoff --to`, `coordinator end`, `todo run`, `resume`, `next`, `stop`, `queue`, `grant`, `revoke`, `worker start`, `prompt`, `interrupt`, `stop`, `kill`, `answer`, `ack` and `escalate`; reads are not fenced, and neither is the user's own answer from their shell or client. `ready` is refused for an agent that is not a claude or pi session, or that is blocked on a question. `coordinator handoff --successor` writes herdr's own snapshot of the tenure (runs in flight with their last event ids, owned workers, pending question ids) and a versioned JSON manifest (epoch, snapshot and note paths with sha256, the old pane and the agent kind) to the state directory, opens a pane in the old pane's tab without moving focus and starts one fresh session of the same agent kind with a single launch prompt that names the manifest (`/todo --handoff PATH` for claude, `/skill:todo --handoff PATH` for pi). Any other agent kind is refused before anything starts. The successor reads the manifest and runs `coordinator ack --epoch N --snapshot SHA256` from its own pane; herdr then advances the epoch, moves the tenure, its workers and runs and the tab's coordinator role to the successor and closes the old pane, which ends the old agent session; if the pane cannot be closed, the reply says so and the old session stays fenced, with `coordinator_superseded`, until that pane closes. A stale or wrong ack is refused (`handoff_refused`) and changes nothing. A successor that is blocked on a permission or trust dialog is starting normally: the handoff stays pending, the dialog shows as a waiting question, and its ack still succeeds. If the successor exits after it was detected, reports a limit or its pane closes before its ack, or `coordinator handoff --abort` runs (from the old pane, the successor's pane, or outside a pane with `--id`), the handoff aborts: the old tenure unfreezes and keeps its authority, the successor's pane is closed (unless the successor itself aborted), and one notice says so. No timer is involved, and `coordinator status` shows a pending handoff's `state` (`frozen` or `successor_started`). A pending handoff is stored with the server's state, so a restart or a live handoff keeps the tenure frozen and the successor's ack valid; if the successor's pane is gone by then, or its agent was detected and then left, the handoff aborts with its one notice; a successor whose agent is not detected yet keeps it pending. A superseded session is fenced by pane and agent session: the old session is refused wherever it runs (`--resume` in another pane), and a new session that reuses the old pane id is not. An ack or an abort whose state write fails is refused and changes nothing. A relative `--note` path is made absolute by the CLI and refused by the server.
+A coordinator can hand its tenure to a fresh session of the same agent without a person in between. `coordinator ready --note FILE` freezes the caller's tenure: herdr copies the note into its state directory and records its sha256. Run `ready` inside the turn and end the turn afterwards. While frozen, every mutation sent from the tenure's pane is refused with `coordinator_frozen`: `coordinator handoff --to`, `coordinator end`, `todo run`, `resume`, `next`, `stop`, `queue`, `grant`, `revoke`, `worker start`, `prompt`, `interrupt`, `stop`, `kill`, `answer`, `ack` and `escalate`; reads are not fenced, and neither is the user's own answer from their shell or client. `ready` is refused for an agent that is not a claude or pi session, or that is blocked on a question. `coordinator handoff --successor` writes herdr's own snapshot of the tenure (runs in flight with their last event ids, owned workers, pending question ids) and a versioned JSON manifest (epoch, snapshot and note paths with sha256, the old pane and the agent kind) to the state directory, opens a pane in the old pane's tab without moving focus and starts one fresh session of the same agent kind with a single launch prompt that names the manifest (`/todo --handoff PATH` for claude, `/skill:todo --handoff PATH` for pi). Any other agent kind is refused before anything starts. The successor reads the manifest and runs `coordinator ack --epoch N --snapshot SHA256` from its own pane; herdr then advances the epoch, moves the tenure, its workers and runs and the tab's coordinator role to the successor and closes the old pane, which ends the old agent session; if the pane cannot be closed, the reply says so and the old session stays fenced, with `coordinator_superseded`, until that pane closes. A stale or wrong ack is refused (`handoff_refused`) and changes nothing. A successor that is blocked on a permission or trust dialog is starting normally: the handoff stays pending, the dialog shows as a waiting question, and its ack still succeeds. If the successor exits after it was detected, reports a limit or its pane closes before its ack, or `coordinator handoff --abort` runs (from the old pane, the successor's pane, or outside a pane with `--id`), the handoff aborts: the old tenure unfreezes and keeps its authority, the successor's pane is closed (unless the successor itself aborted), and one notice says so. No timer is involved, and `coordinator status` shows a pending handoff's `state` (`frozen` or `successor_started`). A pending handoff is stored with the server's state, so a restart or a live handoff keeps the tenure frozen and the successor's ack valid; if the successor's pane is gone by then, or its agent was detected and then left, the handoff aborts with its one notice; a successor whose agent is not detected yet keeps it pending. A superseded session is fenced by pane and agent session: the old session is refused wherever it runs (`--resume` in another pane), and a new session that reuses the old pane id is not. An ack or an abort whose state write fails is refused and changes nothing. `coordinator handoff --successor --funding credits|login` chooses what pays for the successor (default: `[coordinator] funding`, itself `login`, which is today's behaviour: the agent's own login). With `credits` and agent kind `claude`, herdr registers a credit proxy grant bound to the tenure (ledger run `<tenure id>-e<epoch>`, item `coordinator`: the ledger has no stage column, so the stage `coordinator` is encoded as that item) and keeps a loopback listener for it for the successor's whole tenure. The typed command carries no secret: the successor's pane runs herdr's own launcher (`herdr coordinator credit-launch -- claude ...`, not for people), which claims the grant once from the server over the local socket and replaces itself with `claude`, the variables `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=<listener>`, `ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN=<grant>`, `ANTHROPIC_MODEL=<[workers] anthropic_api_model>` and the `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_SONNET_MODEL`, `_OPUS_MODEL` and `_HAIKU_MODEL` aliases (the same model: the proxy admits only that one) set in that process alone. Every other inherited `ANTHROPIC_*` variable, the `CLAUDE_CODE_USE_*` provider switches and Claude Code's OAuth tokens are removed from it, so an API key or login in the environment cannot choose another route. The grant therefore never appears in the pane's echo, scrollback, shell history, environment or argument list, in the start response, the manifest, the snapshot or any state file; the API key never leaves herdr. The grant is held in server memory until the launcher claims it, once, from the successor's pane; a second claim is refused. A login or `apiKeyHelper` set in Claude Code's own settings files is not touched by herdr. The listener and grant end when the successor's tenure ends or the handoff aborts; ending does not wait for a request in flight, which finishes first. The spend counts against `[workers] anthropic_api_cap_usd` and lands in the credit ledger under that run and item; only the standard Sonnet tariff models of `anthropic_api_model` are supported. `credits` is refused, naming why and before anything starts, when the proxy cannot start (no key file, no `anthropic_api_cycle`, no Unix, no socket), the cap is reached or accounting is unavailable, or no usable credit model is configured; it is also refused for `pi`, whose provider file herdr writes only for its own headless workers. A server older than this option answers `unknown_method` to `--funding` instead of starting the successor on the login. `coordinator status` shows each tenure's `funding` and, for a started successor, the handoff's `funding`. The intent (never a grant) is stored with the server's state; the listener lives in the server's memory, so after a server restart or a live handoff a credit session still holds its dead address and its requests fail, never falling back to the login. `coordinator status` then keeps `funding` at `credits` and adds a `funding_note` saying the endpoint is not running and how to recover: start a fresh session on the login and run `coordinator handoff --successor --funding credits` again. Whether the proxy's request allowlist, written for pi, admits every request Claude Code sends has not been established: it stays unchanged (unknown betas and fields remain refused), and the ignored test `installed_claude_code_request_shape_offline` records what the installed Claude Code sends, with a fake upstream and no paid call. A relative `--note` path is made absolute by the CLI and refused by the server.
 
 `coordinator start` claims the coordination of the repository of `--repo` (default: the caller pane's directory) for the caller's pane (`HERDR_PANE_ID`): a tenure with an id of its own (`c-...`). It is refused with `coordinator_active`, naming the other tenure and its pane, while another pane coordinates that repository. `herdr tab role <tab> coordinator` does the same for the tab's focused pane, and the tab's `♛` follows the tenure: `coordinator end` (this pane's tenure, or `--id`'s) or `herdr tab role <tab> none` ends it, and so does closing its pane or exiting its agent. `coordinator status` lists the active tenures, each with the TODO item its `herdr todo run` works on until the run ends. Workers and runs started from a coordinator's pane carry its tenure's id (`owner_coordinator_id`) and belong to the tenure, not to the pane: when the coordinator's agent session comes back in another pane (`claude --resume`), the tenure, its workers, their obligations and its runs move there, and another session in the old pane inherits none of them. A `/clear` in the coordinator's pane keeps the tenure: the agent integration reports the new session (Claude's `SessionStart` with `source: clear`), and the tenure, with its workers and runs, goes on with it, so a later `claude --resume` of the new session finds it. `coordinator handoff --to PANE` passes the coordination on: this pane's tenure (or `--id`'s) ends `handed_off`, and the next one, one epoch on, starts in `PANE` with the item, workers and runs. The repository rooted at the home directory (a dotfiles repository) is claimed only explicitly, with `coordinator start --repo ~`: a pane whose directory is in it, by `coordinator start` without `--repo` or by the coordinator tab role, is refused, while a repository nested in the home directory is claimed as itself. Such a repository often keeps its git directory outside HOME (a bare repository that a `git` wrapper pairs with HOME), which herdr cannot find by looking for `.git`; then `coordinator start` failed with `invalid_request: <HOME> is not in a git repository`. With `--repo ~` the CLI asks git in HOME, with your shell's environment, for that directory (`git rev-parse --git-common-dir`) and sends it; the server checks that it is a repository's common directory that tracks files of HOME, records it in its state directory (`home-repositories.json`, so a restarted or handed-off server keeps it) and runs git there with `--git-dir` and `--work-tree`. Its folder slot lives in `~/.local/state/herdr/worktrees/home/<slot>` (or under `[workers] folder_slots_dir`), never beside HOME, and only once the repository ignores it; its workers write only that slot and their temp directory, never HOME itself or `~/.claude`.
 
